import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.Boholvbs.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Output contract","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/output.md","filePath":"reference/output.md"}'),n={name:"reference/output.md"};function i(r,e,d,h,c,l){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s(`<h1 id="output-contract" tabindex="-1">Output contract <a class="header-anchor" href="#output-contract" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Output contract&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A target turns the resolved program and its facts into the files a robot stack runs. This page fixes what every target&#39;s output guarantees, and then the files of the first target, <code>ros-gz-nav2</code>, of <code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code>, and of <code>mjcf</code>, the robot alone for MuJoCo.</p><h2 id="rules-for-every-target" tabindex="-1">Rules for every target <a class="header-anchor" href="#rules-for-every-target" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rules for every target&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>One directory, runnable by path.</strong> <code>--output DIR</code> receives an ordinary directory. The user builds nothing and installs nothing to run it; the entry point is a launch file inside it, or for <code>mjcf</code> the model MuJoCo opens. A file of the same name is replaced, never written through, so a link in its place is replaced and its target left alone; other files in the directory are left alone. The compiler refuses an output directory that is, or contains, a source file or a library, and a directory inside it that is a symbolic link (<code>environment.output_linked</code>).</p><p><strong>One authoritative file per fact.</strong> A fact appears in the one file whose consumer needs it, and every other file that needs the same fact is derived from the same analysis value at compile time, never re-derived at run time. The table in each target&#39;s section says which file owns which fact.</p><p><strong>No absolute paths.</strong> A file on disk never contains the output directory&#39;s path, so the directory can be moved or mounted elsewhere. Where a consumer demands an absolute path at run time (a simulator plugin that opens a parameter file by name), the file holds the token <code>\${OUTPUT_DIR}</code> and the launch file substitutes the real path when it starts the process.</p><p><strong>Deterministic.</strong> The same program, libraries and compiler version produce byte-identical output: no timestamps, no host names, no random ids.</p><p><strong>Execution input and inspection output are separate.</strong> The runtime reads only the files under <code>robot/</code>, <code>world/</code>, <code>nav/</code>, <code>task/</code>, <code>launch/</code> and <code>runtime/</code>, and of the <code>mjcf</code> target&#39;s output only <code>scene.xml</code>, <code>drive.json</code> and, for a legged base that walks by a policy, the policy beside them that <code>drive.json</code> names (<code>policy.onnx</code>). <code>compile.json</code> is for people and tools: the facts, the validation results, the approximations the target made, and the sources and parts the output came from. Deleting it changes nothing at run time.</p><p><strong>Approximations are declared.</strong> Where the target stands in for something it cannot simulate as such (a mecanum wheel as a sphere with anisotropic friction, a lidar with a default sample count), it says so in <code>compile.json</code> under <code>approximations</code>, with what was approximated, how, and why. Nothing is approximated silently, and nothing the target cannot do is emitted as if it could: an unsupported construct is a <code>target.*</code> diagnostic that stops emission.</p><p><strong>The task stays goal-agnostic in the tree and goal-bound in the mission.</strong> The behavior tree names what to do; the mission file holds the poses the tree&#39;s goals resolve to. A target&#39;s own executor reads both.</p><h2 id="ros-gz-nav2" tabindex="-1"><code>ros-gz-nav2</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#ros-gz-nav2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;\`ros-gz-nav2\`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>ROS 2 Jazzy, Gazebo Harmonic, <code>ros2_control</code>, and Nav2. The output is:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>&lt;output&gt;/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├── robot/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── robot.urdf            links, joints, inertials, visuals, collisions,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   │                         ros2_control interfaces, Gazebo sensor and friction blocks</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── controllers.yaml      controller_manager, the drive controller, joint states;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   │                         not for a base on tracks, which has no joint to control</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── meshes/&lt;part-id&gt;/     the visual meshes, copied from the libraries, each with the</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   │                         files that lie beside it there</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   └── hulls/                &lt;part-id&gt;_hull.stl: each declared hull, as a binary STL,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│                             when a part declares one</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├── world/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── world.sdf             physics, lighting, ground plane, scene entities</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── meshes/&lt;object-id&gt;/   the meshes of the library objects the world places, copied</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   │                         from the libraries with the files beside them; the world</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   │                         names each by its path from here</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   └── bridge.yaml           ros_gz_bridge topics: clock, sensors, a tracked base&#39;s motion</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├── nav/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── nav2.base.yaml        plugin choices and constants</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── nav2.robot.yaml       what the robot decides: footprint, inflation, kinematics, AMCL start</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── map.pgm               the occupancy grid drawn from the world&#39;s obstacles</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   └── map.yaml              its resolution and origin, for the map server</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├── task/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── behavior_tree.xml     the task as a BehaviorTree.CPP tree over the vocabulary&#39;s actions</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── mission.json          every goal and entity by name, the spawn, the time budget</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   └── evaluation.json       the checks, and the planar shapes of the parts and entities</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├── launch/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   └── bringup.launch.py     the entry point: simulator, robot, controllers, navigation, task</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├── runtime/                  the task executor and its adapters, copied here; needs only ROS 2</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>└── compile.json              inspection: facts, validation, approximations, provenance;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                              the library and id of each part, of each placed object and</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                              of each environment a scenario takes</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Nav2 reads parameter files in order with later files winning, so the launch file passes <code>nav2.base.yaml</code> then <code>nav2.robot.yaml</code>. Everything the robot decides is in the second file and nowhere else.</p><p><strong>Status.</strong> Every file above is emitted. The launch brings up the simulator, the robot, the drive controller, Nav2 and the task runtime, in that order, and shuts everything down when the task ends (<code>exit_on_task_end:=false</code> keeps it running; <code>navigation:=false</code> brings up only the simulator, the robot and its drive controller, to drive it by hand on <code>/cmd_vel</code>). It also ends the run, and says what stopped, when a process the base is driven through ends before the launch does: the runtime&#39;s drive node, or the bridge, through which the controllers get the clock a command&#39;s lifetime is measured on. Nothing after these ends a command, so a base whose drive had stopped would run on at its last speeds; a run ended this way writes no result. Gazebo itself takes about five seconds to end. The task runtime writes <code>run/task_result.json</code> under the output directory (<code>run_dir:=DIR</code> moves it); <code>run/</code> is never written by the compiler, so a rerun of the compiler leaves it alone. Navigation localizes on the map drawn from the program&#39;s world, <code>nav/map.yaml</code>, which the map server serves and the global costmap&#39;s static layer reads: a robot with a scan is localized against it by AMCL from the spawn pose; one without has the world frame <code>map</code> meet <code>odom</code> at the spawn pose through a static transform.</p><h3 id="frames-and-topics" tabindex="-1">Frames and topics <a class="header-anchor" href="#frames-and-topics" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Frames and topics&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>base_footprint</code> is the root link: the ground projection of the robot, the frame the spawn pose places, and the controller&#39;s base frame. <code>base_link</code> is the chassis part frame; every other link is named after its component and its joint <code>&lt;component&gt;_joint</code>, and hangs from the link of the part it is mated onto: a wheel from its motor, a motor from the chassis. A wheel joint carries the limits its motor&#39;s part states, its stall torque as the effort and its no-load speed as the velocity. A wheel that a motor turns through a driveline of the chassis hangs from the chassis; its joint carries the motor&#39;s speed over the driveline&#39;s reduction and an equal share of its torque times the reduction, or no limit when the driveline states none. A track&#39;s unit is a fixed link whose one collision is the box of its belt: as long as the belt&#39;s run on the ground and as wide as the belt, standing on the run, as tall as a wheel that travels as far per turn as the belt. No joint and no further link is made for a belt.</li><li>The base takes <code>geometry_msgs/msg/TwistStamped</code> on <code>/cmd_vel</code>, whatever the controller; it publishes odometry on <code>/odom</code> and <code>odom -&gt; base_footprint</code> on <code>/tf</code>. A stale command (older than 0.5 s) stops the base. Mecanum, two-wheel differential, skid-steer, tricycle, Ackermann and omni bases are bound to the matching <code>ros2_control</code> controllers; the part on a steered mount, a motor or a free wheel, hangs from a kingpin joint named <code>&lt;component&gt;_steer_joint</code>, and wheels that only roll are observed but not commanded. Every wheel the program drives is commanded: a binding that left one out is refused. An omni base is bound to <code>velocity_controllers/JointGroupVelocityController</code>, and <code>runtime/run_omni_drive.py</code> turns <code>/cmd_vel</code> into the wheel speeds from each wheel&#39;s position and rolling direction (<code>robot/omni_drive.json</code>) and publishes the odometry from the measured wheel speeds; ros2_controllers&#39; omni controller models only wheels evenly spaced on a circle, and the catalog&#39;s four-wheel omni bases sit on rectangles. An Ackermann base is bound to the same velocity group for its driven wheels, two or four, and to <code>position_controllers/JointGroupPositionController</code>, spawned as <code>steering_controller</code>, for its kingpins; <code>runtime/run_steering_drive.py</code> turns <code>/cmd_vel</code> into each driven wheel&#39;s speed along its own circle about the turning centre and each kingpin&#39;s angle (<code>robot/steering_drive.json</code>), and publishes the odometry from the rear wheels&#39; measured speeds. ros2_controllers&#39; Ackermann controller takes exactly two traction joints. A tracked base has no joint, so the description holds no <code>&lt;ros2_control&gt;</code> block, <code>robot/controllers.yaml</code> is not written and the launch starts no controller manager and spawns no controller. <code>runtime/run_track_drive.py</code> holds <code>/cmd_vel</code> to what the two belts&#39; motors reach, each belt running at the speed of its side, a half separation from the chassis&#39; origin (<code>robot/track_drive.json</code>); it publishes the motion the belts then carry the base in on <code>/tracks/cmd_vel</code> (<code>geometry_msgs/msg/Twist</code>) and the odometry that motion stands for. The bridge hands that topic to Gazebo&#39;s <code>TrackedVehicle</code> system, which tells the <code>TrackController</code> on each belt&#39;s link its speed and the point the base turns about; a belt stops when nothing has reached it for 0.5 s. A chassis whose origin does not lie midway between its two tracks, within 2 mm, is refused.</li><li>A base that steers plans with Smac Hybrid-A* at its minimum turning radius and follows with MPPI&#39;s Ackermann model; it is not held to a final heading, since it cannot turn in place to fix one. The radius is the tightest circle the middle of the unsteered axle follows with every kingpin within its limit: for each kingpin, its distance ahead of that axle over the tangent of its limit, plus its distance from the centre line.</li><li>Sensors publish under their role name: the first laser on <code>/scan</code>, the first spinning lidar on <code>/lidar3d/scan</code> (its middle ring) and <code>/lidar3d/points</code>, the first IMU on <code>/imu</code>, the first camera on <code>/camera/image_raw</code> and <code>/camera/camera_info</code>, the first depth camera on <code>/depth/image_raw</code> and <code>/depth/points</code>, the first RGBD camera on <code>/rgbd/image_raw</code>, <code>/rgbd/depth/image_raw</code>, <code>/rgbd/points</code> and <code>/rgbd/camera_info</code>, the first ultrasonic range finder on <code>/ultrasonic/range</code> (its cone of beams on <code>/ultrasonic/points</code>), the first range finder that measures with light (<code>Range</code>) on <code>/range</code> (its cone on <code>/range/points</code>), the first bumper on <code>/bumper</code> (<code>ros_gz_interfaces/msg/Contacts</code>); a second sensor of a kind is suffixed with its component name. Frames are the component names.</li><li>The costmaps&#39; footprint is the box of the robot&#39;s collision shapes: the analysis footprint, widened to each wheel&#39;s sphere (below), which reaches further across the axle than the wheel&#39;s width.</li><li>The costmaps mark what every point cloud sees, points 0.05 to 2 m above the ground, and with the localizer read one scan. It is the first laser&#39;s; without a laser it is the first spinning lidar&#39;s middle ring; a robot with neither but with a depth or RGBD camera gets <code>/scan</code> from <code>runtime/run_depth_scan.py</code>, which projects the cloud onto the ground plane in <code>base_footprint</code> (points 0.05 to 2 m high, within 5 m, 360 beams, beams outside the camera&#39;s view infinite). Range finders feed the costmaps&#39; range layer: <code>runtime/run_range_sensor.py</code> reports each cone&#39;s nearest return as a <code>sensor_msgs/msg/Range</code>, <code>ULTRASOUND</code> or <code>INFRARED</code>, and a reading at the maximum range clears the cone. An ultrasonic sensor&#39;s returns less than 0.05 m above the ground are dropped first (every simulated beam returns, a pulse glancing off the floor does not); a <code>Range</code> sensor keeps them, since light comes back from the floor. A <code>Range</code> sensor whose limits are equal says only whether something is within that distance: it reports <code>-inf</code> or <code>+inf</code>, and the range layer marks or clears its cone. A robot with no range sensor is warned that it navigates blind.</li><li>The simulator publishes the model pose on <code>/model/&lt;robot&gt;/pose</code> as <code>tf2_msgs/msg/TFMessage</code>. Tests and the evaluator judge a run by this, never by odometry.</li></ul><h3 id="who-owns-which-fact" tabindex="-1">Who owns which fact <a class="header-anchor" href="#who-owns-which-fact" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Who owns which fact&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Authoritative file</th><th>Also derived into</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Link and joint geometry, inertials</td><td><code>robot/robot.urdf</code></td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Wheel radius, track width, wheelbase</td><td><code>robot/controllers.yaml</code> (the controller&#39;s kinematics); for a runtime drive its layout, <code>robot/omni_drive.json</code>, <code>robot/steering_drive.json</code> or <code>robot/track_drive.json</code></td><td><code>nav2.robot.yaml</code> velocity limits</td></tr><tr><td>Footprint</td><td><code>nav/nav2.robot.yaml</code> (both costmaps, once each)</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Wheel joint names and command interfaces</td><td><code>robot/robot.urdf</code> (<code>&lt;ros2_control&gt;</code>)</td><td><code>controllers.yaml</code> names the same joints</td></tr><tr><td>Sensor topics and frames</td><td><code>robot/robot.urdf</code> (<code>&lt;gazebo&gt;</code> sensor blocks)</td><td><code>world/bridge.yaml</code>, <code>nav2.robot.yaml</code> observation sources</td></tr><tr><td>Spawn pose</td><td><code>task/mission.json</code></td><td>the launch file&#39;s spawn arguments; AMCL&#39;s initial pose in <code>nav2.robot.yaml</code></td></tr><tr><td>Obstacle positions, sizes and, for a library object, the shapes it collides by</td><td><code>world/world.sdf</code></td><td><code>nav/map.pgm</code> rasterized from the same values; <code>task/evaluation.json</code></td></tr><tr><td>Goal and entity positions</td><td><code>task/mission.json</code></td><td><code>nav/map.pgm</code>&#39;s extent; <code>task/evaluation.json</code>&#39;s entity regions</td></tr><tr><td>Gravity, scene entities</td><td><code>world/world.sdf</code></td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Time budget</td><td><code>task/mission.json</code></td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="what-the-launch-file-substitutes-at-run-time" tabindex="-1">What the launch file substitutes at run time <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-launch-file-substitutes-at-run-time" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the launch file substitutes at run time&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>\${OUTPUT_DIR}</code> in <code>robot/robot.urdf</code> (the path of <code>controllers.yaml</code> for <code>gz_ros2_control</code>, and the mesh paths). <code>world/world.sdf</code> holds nothing to substitute: it names an object&#39;s mesh by its path from the world file, <code>meshes/&lt;object-id&gt;/&lt;file&gt;</code>, which Gazebo resolves against that file.</li><li>The path of <code>nav/map.yaml</code>, passed to the map server.</li><li>The spawn pose, read from <code>task/mission.json</code>, for the simulator and, on a robot without a laser, for the <code>map -&gt; odom</code> transform.</li></ul><p>Nothing else is computed at launch; the launch file is a fixed program over the files above. Nav2&#39;s own default behavior tree runs <code>Navigate</code>; the program&#39;s tree is not handed to <code>bt_navigator</code>.</p><h3 id="the-task" tabindex="-1">The task <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-task" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The task&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>task/behavior_tree.xml</code> is the task in BehaviorTree.CPP&#39;s XML layout with the language&#39;s node names as tags and its parameter names as attributes, so it reads like the program and opens in Groot. A goal is written by name (<code>&lt;Navigate goal=&quot;dock&quot;/&gt;</code>) and resolved through <code>task/mission.json</code>, which holds every goal and every entity, since <code>Navigate</code> and <code>GoalReached</code> take either. A task whose body has several nodes runs them in order, so the body is wrapped in a <code>Sequence</code>.</p><p><code>runtime/run_task.py</code> runs the tree. It needs ROS 2 and, when the tree uses <code>Navigate</code>, <code>Spin</code>, <code>Advance</code> or <code>BackUp</code>, the task waits for a Nav2 costmap or the runtime starts Nav2&#39;s servers, the <code>nav2_msgs</code> package; nothing from the compiler. Each leaf is bound as follows.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Node</th><th>Bound to</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Navigate</code></td><td>Nav2 <code>navigate_to_pose</code>, goal in the <code>map</code> frame</td></tr><tr><td><code>Spin</code></td><td>Nav2 <code>spin</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Advance</code>, <code>BackUp</code></td><td>Nav2 <code>drive_on_heading</code>, <code>backup</code>; 0.15 m/s unless <code>speed_mps</code> is given</td></tr><tr><td><code>Strafe</code></td><td><code>/cmd_vel</code> with odometry feedback (Nav2 has no lateral behavior)</td></tr><tr><td><code>Wait</code></td><td>simulation time</td></tr><tr><td><code>Stop</code></td><td>zero velocity on <code>/cmd_vel</code> for half a second</td></tr><tr><td><code>GoalReached</code></td><td><code>map -&gt; base_footprint</code> within the goal tolerance of <code>nav2.base.yaml</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>DistanceTraveled</code></td><td>odometry path length since the task started</td></tr><tr><td><code>TimeExpired</code></td><td>simulation time since the task started</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The launch starts Nav2&#39;s lifecycle manager without <code>autostart</code>. The task runtime asks it to start the servers, 3 s of wall time or more after the manager first answered it (<code>run_task.py --start-lifecycle</code>, <code>--start-after</code>), and waits until the manager reports them active. The manager answers once it has made its clients of the servers, and the wait gives the servers more time to discover those clients. rmw_fastrtps gives a server&#39;s reply up when the manager&#39;s client has not matched it within the time the reply may block, and Nav2 Jazzy&#39;s manager waits for a <code>change_state</code> reply without a limit. The 3 s are a measured remedy, not a guarantee. After a start that failed the manager reports its servers not active, so the task does not start. When the runtime receives the manager&#39;s answer that it could not start its servers, the run ends with status <code>not_started</code> and says so, without waiting for the limit below.</p><p>The task starts once the drive&#39;s odometry and the robot&#39;s pose in the map have arrived, Nav2&#39;s lifecycle manager reports its servers active, and the task has received from the local and the global costmap their map and the robot&#39;s footprint, which Nav2 checks a goal against (so that Nav2&#39;s servers most likely have them too); one that cannot start within 120 s of simulation time ends with status <code>not_started</code> and says what it waited for. The tree ticks at ten hertz. The mission&#39;s <code>timeout_s</code> ends the run with status <code>timeout</code>. <code>run/task_result.json</code> records the status (<code>success</code>, <code>failure</code>, <code>timeout</code>, <code>error</code>, <code>not_started</code>), the start and end times, one event per leaf with its parameters and outcome, the final pose the robot believes it has, and the odometry distance. It is the robot&#39;s own account. A halted Nav2 action is canceled at once, and before the run is judged the runtime waits up to 5 s for the goal to stop; a goal that has not stopped by then is named in <code>unstopped_actions</code>.</p><h3 id="the-evaluation" tabindex="-1">The evaluation <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-evaluation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The evaluation&quot;">​</a></h3><p>While the task runs, the runtime records the robot&#39;s pose as the simulator publishes it, the joint states, and every bumper&#39;s contact reports. When the task ends it writes <code>run/evaluation.json</code> with three verdicts that rest on different evidence:</p><ul><li><code>task</code>: the status the task runtime reported.</li><li><code>physical</code>: from the recorded ground truth alone. Whether the robot ended near the last goal it navigated to (within twice the controller&#39;s goal tolerance, since the controller stops on its belief and the ground truth differs from it by the localization error), and its closest approach to any <code>Obstacle</code> over the run; touching one (within 5 mm) fails.</li><li><code>checks</code>: each check of the <code>.eval</code> file, <code>pass</code>, <code>fail</code> or <code>unknown</code>, with the value it was judged on. <code>Check_vel</code>, <code>Check_acc</code>, <code>Check_jerk</code> and <code>Check_effort</code> read the joint states of the named wheel and judge every sample by magnitude; <code>Check_distance</code> and <code>Check_clearance</code> use the recorded poses and the planar shapes of <code>task/evaluation.json</code>; <code>Check_contact</code> on a <code>Bumper</code> reads the bumper&#39;s own reports (the seconds it was pressed and the scene entities that pressed it), on any other part the poses and shapes; <code>Check_time</code> uses the task&#39;s duration. The <a href="./vocabulary.html">vocabulary</a> states what each bound means.</li></ul><p>The shapes are the collision geometry the robot is simulated with, seen from above: the chassis and sensor collision boxes, each wheel and each ground contact a part declares as a circle of its radius (both are spheres in the simulator), a part without a collision box as a point, and each scene entity as the regions its shapes cover seen from above at its full pose, so that a cylinder lying on its side covers its length: one region for a primitive, and for a library object one for each shape it collides by, a robot being as near to it as to the nearest of them. An object that declares no collision has no region and is not listed. The map is drawn from the same regions. A part&#39;s ground contacts count as that part: a check on the chassis also measures the skid under it. Nothing is read from odometry or from Nav2. A check on a part without a moving joint is refused at compile time; a check that has no samples to judge is <code>unknown</code>, never <code>pass</code>.</p><h3 id="approximations-this-target-makes" tabindex="-1">Approximations this target makes <a class="header-anchor" href="#approximations-this-target-makes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Approximations this target makes&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Fact in the program</th><th>What the target emits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A mecanum wheel</td><td>A sphere collision of the wheel&#39;s radius with <code>mu = 1</code>, <code>mu2 = 0</code> and <code>fdir1</code> along the axis of the roller under the hub, expressed in <code>base_footprint</code>: <code>(1, -1, 0)</code> for the front-left and rear-right mounts, <code>(1, 1, 0)</code> for the other two. It grips along that axis and slides freely across it, as the roller turns</td><td>Gazebo&#39;s DART engine has no roller model; the sphere gives one contact point, and the friction direction gives the roller its axis</td></tr><tr><td>A driven standard wheel on a two-wheel base</td><td>A sphere collision of the wheel&#39;s radius with isotropic friction <code>mu = mu2 = 3</code></td><td>One contact point keeps the controller&#39;s odometry honest; a two-wheel base turns by wheel speed difference and needs lateral grip</td></tr><tr><td>An omni wheel</td><td>A sphere collision of the wheel&#39;s radius with <code>mu = 1</code> along its rolling direction (the declared axle turned a quarter turn about up, expressed in <code>base_footprint</code>) and <code>mu2 = 0</code> along its axle</td><td>The rollers along the rim let the wheel slide along its axle; the sphere gives one contact point</td></tr><tr><td>A driven standard wheel on a skid-steer base</td><td>A sphere collision with <code>mu = 1</code> across its rolling direction (<code>fdir1</code> along its axle, <code>0 1 0</code> in <code>base_footprint</code>) and <code>mu2 = 3</code> along it, and Gazebo&#39;s WheelSlip system with a sideways slip compliance of 2, multiplied by (wheelbase / track)² for a base longer than it is wide, at an equal share of the robot&#39;s weight</td><td>A skid-steer base turns by dragging its wheels sideways. With rigid sideways grip it swung about its more heavily loaded axle; a sideways force that grows with the slip, scaled alike for every wheel, lets it turn about its middle while every wheel carries at least half the average load. WheelSlip takes the first friction direction to be the axle. The compliance is the previous compiler&#39;s (<code>icra2027-submission</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>A passive wheel (caster)</td><td>A sphere collision with <code>mu = mu2 = 0.01</code></td><td>A support must slide in every direction</td></tr><tr><td>The ground plane and each <code>SupportSurface</code></td><td>A collision with <code>mu = mu2 = 3</code>, the highest coefficient the target&#39;s contact models state</td><td>Gazebo&#39;s DART engine gives a contact the smaller of its two surfaces&#39; coefficients, so a floor at the default 1 would hold every wheel to 1 and the AgileX LIMO PRO, a four-wheel skid-steer base 200 mm long and 173 mm wide, did not turn in place</td></tr><tr><td>A track</td><td>A box on its run on the ground, as long as the run, as wide as the belt and as tall as a wheel that travels as far per turn as the belt, whose surface moves to carry the base and grips with <code>mu = mu2 = 1</code>; the rest of the unit collides with nothing</td><td>The library states the belt&#39;s run, width and travel, not its shape around its wheels nor its grip</td></tr><tr><td>A track&#39;s drive</td><td>A belt held to the speed its motor reaches unloaded; where the speed it can reach is not known (no <code>Motor</code> turns it, or its driveline states no reduction), a belt whose speed nothing bounds</td><td>A moving contact surface takes a speed, not a force: the belt&#39;s pull is bounded by the floor&#39;s grip, not by its motor&#39;s stall torque</td></tr><tr><td>A tracked base</td><td>A base whose belts carry it about the centre its command turns it about, without scrubbing sideways as they turn it; its odometry is made from the speeds its belts are given</td><td>How far a belt slips sideways in a turn follows from its pressure along its run and its grip, which the library does not state; neither simulator gives a belt a turning hub to measure</td></tr><tr><td>A tracked base, in Gazebo</td><td>A base that turns in place short of its command when its tracks&#39; runs are long for the distance between them: 0.83 of it with runs of 0.15 m that lie 0.16 m apart, 1.02 with runs of 0.10 m</td><td>Gazebo&#39;s <code>TrackController</code> moves every contact of a belt at the belt&#39;s one speed about the centre of rotation, so a contact further from that centre than the middle of its belt drags</td></tr><tr><td>A ground contact a part declares (<code>contacts</code>)</td><td>A sphere collision of its radius on a fixed, massless link of its own, <code>&lt;link&gt;_contact_&lt;n&gt;</code>, with <code>mu = mu2 = 0.01</code>; the footprint reaches around it</td><td>Like a caster, a support must slide in every direction; the statics rule counts it, so the simulator has it</td></tr><tr><td>A steered wheel</td><td>A 10 g knuckle link between the chassis and the wheel, turning about the declared kingpin within its limit, with an effort limit of 50 N m and a speed limit of 3 rad/s</td><td>The library declares the kingpin&#39;s axis and limit but no knuckle body or steering actuator</td></tr><tr><td>An Ackermann base</td><td>Each driven wheel held to the speed of its own path about the turning centre, and each kingpin to its own angle, by the runtime&#39;s steering drive</td><td>The library states which wheels a motor turns and how far a kingpin turns, not the differential and the steering linkage that join them</td></tr><tr><td>A wheel one motor turns together with others through a driveline</td><td>A wheel joint limited to an equal share of the motor&#39;s stall torque times the driveline&#39;s reduction</td><td>The chassis part states the driveline&#39;s reduction, not how its differential shares the torque between the wheels</td></tr><tr><td>A wheel on a driveline that states no reduction</td><td>A wheel joint without effort or velocity limits</td><td>What the motor can do at the wheel is not known</td></tr><tr><td>A laser</td><td>A <code>gpu_lidar</code> with 360 beams over the full circle, 0.3 to 12 m, at 10 Hz</td><td>The library does not yet state sensor specifications</td></tr><tr><td>A spinning lidar</td><td>A <code>gpu_lidar</code> with 16 rings of 360 beams over 0.52 rad, 0.3 to 20 m, at 10 Hz</td><td>As above</td></tr><tr><td>An IMU</td><td>An ideal <code>imu</code> sensor at 100 Hz</td><td>As above</td></tr><tr><td>A camera</td><td>A 320 by 240 <code>camera</code>, 1.089 rad field of view, at 10 Hz</td><td>As above</td></tr><tr><td>A depth camera</td><td>A 160 by 120 <code>depth_camera</code>, 1.089 rad field of view, 0.1 to 5 m, at 10 Hz</td><td>As above; at 320 by 240 the clouds overran ROS transport and navigation stopped on stale transforms</td></tr><tr><td>An RGBD camera</td><td>A 160 by 120 <code>rgbd_camera</code>, 1.089 rad field of view, 0.1 to 5 m, at 10 Hz</td><td>As above</td></tr><tr><td>An ultrasonic range finder</td><td>A <code>gpu_lidar</code> of 9 by 9 beams filling a 0.52 rad cone, 0.02 to 4 m, at 20 Hz, whose nearest return is the reading; the cone, limits and rate its part states, when it does</td><td>The simulator has no range finder</td></tr><tr><td>A range finder that measures with light (<code>Range</code>)</td><td>A <code>gpu_lidar</code> of 9 by 9 beams filling the cone its part states, from its minimum (from 0.01 m for a fixed-distance sensor or a minimum of zero) to its maximum, at its rate, whose nearest return is the reading</td><td>The simulator has no range finder</td></tr><tr><td>An ultrasonic range finder&#39;s reading</td><td>Made at run time from the cone&#39;s returns more than 0.05 m above the ground</td><td>A pulse glancing off the floor does not echo; every simulated beam returns</td></tr><tr><td>A bumper</td><td>An ideal <code>contact</code> sensor over its collision box, read at 50 Hz; the bumper keeps its own link so that the sensor can name the collision</td><td>The library does not state the sensor&#39;s specification</td></tr><tr><td>A spinning lidar as the only planar range sensor</td><td>Its middle ring as the scan the costmaps and the localizer read</td><td>Nav2 and AMCL read laser scans</td></tr><tr><td>A depth or RGBD camera as the only range sensor</td><td>Its cloud projected onto a planar scan for the costmaps and the localizer</td><td>As above</td></tr><tr><td>A part without a collision box</td><td>Its visual mesh as collision geometry</td><td>Nothing else is known</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The friction values are the ones the previous compiler drove its experiments with. Each approximation is listed in <code>compile.json</code> for the component that triggered it.</p><h3 id="not-emitted" tabindex="-1">Not emitted <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-emitted" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not emitted&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A program that uses any of these stops with a <code>target.unsupported</code> diagnostic naming the construct, and nothing is written: an omni base whose rolling directions do not span the plane, a steering base whose chassis declares no kingpin, a scene entity declared movable (<code>is_static = false</code>: an entity states no mass to move it with), a joint check on a part without a moving joint (a track among them: its belt is a moving surface), a part with links of its own, which of the targets only <code>mjcf</code> writes, a moving joint (<code>revolute</code>, <code>prismatic</code> or <code>continuous</code>) on a connector a mating uses, other than a wheel&#39;s own: on the mating part&#39;s own connector unless the part is a wheel, or on the connector it mates onto, either of which the robot description would hold fixed, a track whose part states no travel per turn or whose run does not lie along the robot, a tracked base whose tracks do not lie equally far to the two sides of its chassis&#39; origin, a component named like a link the target adds: <code>base_footprint</code>, <code>base_link</code>, a declared ground contact&#39;s <code>&lt;link&gt;_contact_&lt;n&gt;</code> or a steered wheel&#39;s knuckle <code>&lt;wheel&gt;_steer</code>, and a scene entity named like another model of the world: the robot, or <code>ground_plane</code> when the scenario has a floor.</p><h2 id="the-ros-mujoco-nav2-target" tabindex="-1">The <code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code> target <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-ros-mujoco-nav2-target" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The \`ros-mujoco-nav2\` target&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The second target keeps everything above that is ROS: the robot description (<code>robot/robot.urdf</code>, with a <code>&lt;mujoco&gt;</code> compiler block MuJoCo&#39;s URDF reader takes and none of Gazebo&#39;s blocks), the controllers, the navigation parameters, the map, the task files and the runtime, and swaps the simulator. Its <code>world/</code> holds <code>mujoco.json</code> instead of an SDF and a bridge: the ground, the world&#39;s <code>light</code> (the Gazebo world&#39;s, below), the scene entities (each a body <code>&lt;name&gt;::link</code>, so that no entity name can meet a link&#39;s in MuJoCo&#39;s single namespace, with its <code>shapes</code>: the primitive a program states, or the shapes a library object collides by, each placed in the entity&#39;s frame; and for a library object drawn in glTF its <code>visual</code>, the mesh&#39;s file in the output and where it sits in the entity&#39;s frame, the mesh lying under <code>world/meshes/&lt;object-id&gt;/</code> as in the Gazebo target), the actuators on the wheel and kingpin joints, a tracked base&#39;s belts and the topic their motion arrives on, the wheels&#39; contact friction, the sensors with the topics they publish on, and the spawn pose. Each body weighs what the library&#39;s inertial states: the compiler block stops MuJoCo from inferring a mass from a link&#39;s collision shapes, so a link without an inertial (the root, a ground contact&#39;s) weighs only MuJoCo&#39;s 1 g bound. <code>runtime/run_mujoco_sim.py</code> loads the description and that scene, steps the physics paced to the wall clock, publishes <code>/clock</code>, and speaks to ros2_control through its topic-based hardware (<code>/mujoco/joint_states</code>, <code>/mujoco/joint_commands</code>). It publishes the robot&#39;s pose on <code>/model/&lt;robot&gt;/pose</code> and every sensor on the same topics as the Gazebo target, computed from the physics state: lasers and range finders cast rays, which return from what collides (the robot never sees itself, and the floor returns only to a <code>Range</code> sensor, which measures with light), cameras render offscreen (OSMesa by default, <code>MUJOCO_GL=egl</code> with a GPU) what is shown: the sky, the floor and each entity, by its shape or by its object&#39;s mesh, a mark that only shows among them, and never the robot&#39;s own collision shapes. A camera draws from the near end of its range to the far end and shows the sky beyond, as a Gazebo camera does, also in the colour picture of a camera that measures depth. The far end of a camera that measures depth is a distance from it, 5 m, and that of a plain camera a distance ahead of it, 10 m, as in Gazebo; an IMU reads the body&#39;s motion, a bumper reads the contacts. The launch takes the same arguments; <code>headless</code> has no effect.</p><p>Both targets light a world alike, since a program states no light: an ambient light of 0.4, a sun of 1 that shines along (-0.5, 0.1, -0.9) and casts shadows, a sky of (0.7, 0.75, 0.85), a floor of 0.8 grey and, for a box, a sphere or a cylinder a program states, a grey of 0.5. <code>world/world.sdf</code> states them as they are. Gazebo encodes its picture, each value raised to 1 / 2.2, and MuJoCo draws the numbers it is given, so <code>world/mujoco.json</code> holds the colours encoded (<code>background</code>, <code>ground</code>, <code>shape</code>, and <code>gamma</code> for the node to encode a mesh&#39;s material colours by) and the sun at half its strength, at which the robot&#39;s colour camera read one world alike in the two test images: the sky (217, 223, 235) and (214, 221, 234) of 255, the lit floor 188 to 209 and 196, a picture&#39;s red (76, 5, 8) and (79, 14, 15), a stated box&#39;s side away from the sun 71 and 74. The node draws the sun&#39;s shadows in four times the depth MuJoCo would, since the lit floor showed dark specks short of the horizon otherwise.</p><p>The node runs on MuJoCo 3.11 or later and says so on an older one. A tracked base is simulated through the surface velocity of a geom, which MuJoCo has had since 3.11: the node takes the motion on <code>/tracks/cmd_vel</code> and moves the surface of each belt&#39;s box as that motion requires at every point of it, along the run at the speed of its side and across it as the turn swings the point round, so that what touches the floor stands still on it. No controller manager runs for such a base.</p><p>The launch ends the run, and says what stopped, when the runtime&#39;s drive node or the controller manager ends before it does: a group controller holds the last command its drive gave it, and the MuJoCo node the last one the controller manager gave it.</p><p>A geom&#39;s friction in MuJoCo is the same in every direction, and the node writes no contact pairs, so a sphere cannot stand in for a mecanum or omni wheel, which slides freely across its rolling direction on its rollers: a base on such wheels is refused with <code>target.unsupported</code> and compiles for <code>ros-gz-nav2</code> instead (the <code>mjcf</code> target writes a mecanum wheel as its rollers). A part that declares neither a collision box nor a hull is refused too, unless it is a wheel, which collides as a sphere of its radius, or a track, which collides as its belt&#39;s box: the Gazebo target collides such a part through its visual mesh, which the library ships as Collada, and MuJoCo reads meshes only as STL, OBJ or MSH. A library object that only shows, by a mesh that is not glTF, is refused as well: the node reads glTF and nothing else of a world&#39;s objects, and nothing of this one would be in the scene. The world&#39;s gravity and ground plane are the program&#39;s, as in Gazebo, and so is the refusal of an entity declared movable. A library environment is in neither target&#39;s files as such: its entities are the scenario&#39;s own by the time a target reads the world. Its own stand-ins, declared in <code>compile.json</code>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Construct</th><th>Emitted</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A wheel</td><td>A sphere contact of the wheel&#39;s radius with isotropic friction 1, 0.01 for a wheel that does not roll</td><td>MuJoCo&#39;s contact friction is isotropic; a rolling wheel grips, a caster slides</td></tr><tr><td>A ground contact a part declares</td><td>A sphere contact of its radius on the Gazebo target&#39;s fixed link, with isotropic friction 0.01</td><td>Like a caster, a support slides in every direction</td></tr><tr><td>The drive</td><td>A velocity actuator on each driven wheel, of gain 0.5 N m s/rad, or its share of the base&#39;s weight at its rim over 20 ms where that is more, its force clamped to the motor&#39;s stall torque and its command to the motor&#39;s no-load speed; each driven wheel&#39;s joint carrying 1e-4 kg m² of its motor&#39;s turning inertia; and a position actuator of gains 50 N m/rad and 2 N m s/rad on each kingpin</td><td>A part states its motor&#39;s ratings, not the driver&#39;s loop gain or the motor&#39;s inertia behind its gearbox. At 0.5 the heaviest bases barely moved and did not turn; without the inertia a base on wheels of a few grams turned in place faster than it was told and, driven ahead at once, spun round</td></tr><tr><td>An ultrasonic range finder</td><td>Its reading from the cone&#39;s nearest return, the floor never returning</td><td>A pulse glancing off the floor does not echo; the rays skip it</td></tr><tr><td>A skid-steer base</td><td>A base that turns slower than commanded, the demo skid-steer base in place at about a third of the rate, its wheel odometry, taken from its wheels&#39; turning, reporting the commanded turn</td><td>MuJoCo&#39;s isotropic friction resists a wheel dragged across its rolling direction as much as it grips along it</td></tr><tr><td>A library object</td><td>Its mesh for the cameras, in its materials&#39; base colours and the PNG pictures laid on them, without their emission and their other maps; the shapes it collides by for contact and for the lasers&#39; and range finders&#39; rays, not drawn where the mesh is. Of an object whose mesh is not glTF, those shapes, shown in the mesh&#39;s place</td><td>MuJoCo decodes STL, OBJ and MSH, not the glTF a library&#39;s objects are drawn in, so the node reads glTF itself and hands MuJoCo the triangles and pictures; its rays are cast at what collides</td></tr><tr><td>A mark that only shows, a <code>Marker</code> that does not collide</td><td>Its shape or its object&#39;s mesh for the cameras; nothing of it for the lasers and range finders</td><td>The node casts its rays at what collides, where Gazebo&#39;s lasers and range finders see what is shown</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The stand-ins of a driveline, of an Ackermann base and of a track and a tracked base are the Gazebo target&#39;s, since both targets share the robot description and the drive; the tracked base&#39;s shortfall in a turn is Gazebo&#39;s alone.</p><p>The test image is <code>robotdsl/ros-mujoco-nav2:jazzy</code>, built from <code>docker/ros-mujoco-nav2/jazzy/Dockerfile</code>.</p><h2 id="the-mjcf-target" tabindex="-1">The <code>mjcf</code> target <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-mjcf-target" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The \`mjcf\` target&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The third target writes the robot alone, for MuJoCo without ROS: the model MuJoCo opens by itself, its viewer or MuJoCo compiled to WebAssembly in a browser page, and what drives it. It is the robot to drive by hand.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>File</th><th>What it holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>scene.xml</code></td><td>The robot on its floor as one MJCF, which names no file</td></tr><tr><td><code>drive.json</code></td><td>How a base motion becomes each actuator&#39;s command or each belt&#39;s surface motion, or how a legged base&#39;s walking controller is called, which body each component and each of its parts&#39; links is, and each servo</td></tr><tr><td><code>policy.onnx</code></td><td>A legged base&#39;s policy, when it walks by one, copied as its part states it</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>scene.xml</code> is the robot <code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code> gives MuJoCo, as MuJoCo&#39;s URDF reader makes the robot description into a model under that target&#39;s compiler block, written out: a body for each link where its joint puts it, a hinge for each continuous or revolute joint, bounded in force by its limit&#39;s effort, each body&#39;s inertial as the robot description gives it, the steering knuckles it adds included, within MuJoCo&#39;s bounds on mass and inertia, and each collision as a geom, a declared hull as a mesh written in the model with its faces wound outward, as its STL has them. Around it is what that target&#39;s node adds at run time: a free joint at the world&#39;s spawn pose, the robot&#39;s geoms in group 1, which meet the world and not each other, the friction of each wheel, belt and ground contact, the world&#39;s gravity and its floor, the step of 1 ms, the implicit-fast integrator, a velocity actuator on each joint the drive turns and a position actuator on each kingpin it steers, named <code>&lt;joint&gt;_actuator</code>, and the node&#39;s lighting: its headlight, its sun, its sky and the depth of the sun&#39;s shadows. Of the world there is the floor and the gravity and nothing else; a sensor is its body, mass and collision, without its sensing; nothing of ROS, Nav2 or the task.</p><p>The tests build the model both ways, by this target and by that target&#39;s own node from its output, and compare in MuJoCo 3.15 every field of their bodies, joints, degrees of freedom, geoms, actuators, light and sky, their options and their drawing settings. Set aside are the world&#39;s entities, the cameras the node adds for the robot&#39;s sensors and the nearest distance it has them draw at, the light&#39;s offset from the world&#39;s centre of mass, which the entities weigh in, and the offsets that place an element in its model&#39;s arrays: of these, each body&#39;s parent, each joint&#39;s and each geom&#39;s body and each actuator&#39;s joint are compared by name, and the others, which MuJoCo derives from them, are left out. A mesh is compared by its triangles and their normals in its body&#39;s frame, since MuJoCo keeps a mesh in a frame it chooses by its shape. Each hull part&#39;s mesh is a file of its own, named by its part, so the two agree for any number of hull parts.</p><p>A mecanum wheel, which <code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code> refuses, is written as its rollers: its body is the hub, with no shape of its own, and round it are its rollers, each turning freely on a hinge about its own axis that nothing drives. The roller under the hub lies at 45 degrees to the axle, on whichever of the two such lines the mount&#39;s roller axis as <code>ros-gz-nav2</code> states it is nearer (seen from above, <code>(1, -1)</code> at the front-left and rear-right mounts, <code>(1, 1)</code> at the other two), and the others follow it round. A roller is a barrel, as a mecanum wheel&#39;s are: five spheres along its axis, each reaching the wheel&#39;s radius from the axle, so that the rollers keep to the wheel&#39;s radius but for the dips between their spheres. At its middle a roller&#39;s radius is a quarter of the wheel&#39;s radius or of the rollers&#39; span, whichever is less; the rollers span 95% of the wheel&#39;s width along the axle; and there are as many as make their spans overlap round the rim by a tenth, twelve at the least. A wheel is refused whose rollers&#39; end spheres would be less than a fifth of their middle radius (a wheel wider than about two thirds of its diameter, 0.666 of it), or that would need more than 32 rollers (one narrower than about a ninth of it, 0.108). Each roller weighs 1 g with a moment of 1e-6 kg m² about each axis, the least the compiler block takes, out of the wheel&#39;s mass; a wheel whose hub would be left less than 1 g is refused. The hub keeps the rest of the mass and the part&#39;s inertia, so the wheel turns about its axle a little heavier than the part states. The rollers meet the floor with a rolling wheel&#39;s friction, and not the robot; each roller&#39;s body and hinge are named <code>&lt;wheel&#39;s link&gt;/roller_&lt;n&gt;</code>, a name no component can have.</p><p>A part&#39;s own links, which <code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code> refuses, are written below the part&#39;s body, whose inertial is the part&#39;s base alone, each after the link it hangs from: a body named <code>&lt;component&gt;/&lt;link&gt;</code>, a name no component can have, where its joint puts it; for a revolute joint, a hinge of that name about the joint&#39;s axis within its range (a limit MuJoCo holds as a soft constraint), with the <code>damping</code>, <code>armature</code> and <code>frictionloss</code> its servo states, and a position actuator, <code>&lt;component&gt;/&lt;link&gt;_actuator</code>, of the servo&#39;s <code>kp</code>, its force bounded by the servo&#39;s <code>effort</code> and its command by the joint&#39;s range, every number of the link written exactly (rounded, a narrow range closed to a point, which MuJoCo reads as no range, and a turn or an armature to nine digits was not the model a walking controller was trained on); a fixed joint, no joint; the link&#39;s inertial, a diagonal tensor as it is and any other as its principal moments about its principal axes (<code>diaginertia</code> and <code>quat</code>), so that MuJoCo does not decompose the tensor again (its decomposition of a thin plate&#39;s tensor turned off the axes broke the triangle inequality by a rounding, and it refused the model), a link lighter than 1 g, or a principal moment the decomposition finds less than 1e-6 kg m², written raised to those bounds, to which MuJoCo raises every body, and then the largest moment written as the sum of the other two where the decomposition&#39;s rounding put it above, since MuJoCo checks the numbers written; and its shapes, a box by its half sizes, a sphere by its radius, a cylinder by its radius and half its height and a capsule by its radius and half the distance between its ends&#39; centres, in group 1 with the robot&#39;s other shapes, meeting the world and not the robot. MuJoCo starts a model at its joints&#39; zero. When some links turn on servos, the keyframe <code>hold</code> has the free joint at the spawn pose, each servo&#39;s hinge and command at the servo&#39;s <code>hold</code>, written exactly, and every other joint and command at zero, so a program that resets to it (<code>mj_resetDataKeyframe</code>) starts the robot as its servos hold it when nothing commands them; a robot whose links are all fixed has no servo and no keyframe. A legged base is written to walk by the walking controller its part states, or by the walker trained for its body and its legs as they are placed; without either, to stand, the model a walker is trained on. No shape of the robot meets anything, the chassis&#39;s and every part&#39;s in group 1 with <code>contype</code> and <code>conaffinity</code> 0, and each foot meets the floor by a contact pair of its own, the foot&#39;s shape named after its part, the chassis or the leg it is a foot of, and the foot with a slash, of three dimensions, with the foot&#39;s friction sliding and MuJoCo&#39;s own for turning and rolling (0.005, 0.0001), as in the models walking policies are trained on. The model&#39;s free body is the chassis itself, as in those models, with no footprint body below it that MuJoCo&#39;s compiler would weigh at its least mass, and its inertial is written exactly, as its links&#39; are. The keyframe stands the robot on its feet at the spawn pose. Under a policy, the model steps as the policy was trained, its <code>&lt;option&gt;</code> the controller&#39;s <code>simulator</code> (its step, written exactly, so that the period is a whole number of steps as the library states it, its integrator, the solver&#39;s iterations and line-search iterations, and Euler&#39;s implicit damping, off as a <code>&lt;flag&gt;</code>); the policy&#39;s IMU is a site named <code>imu</code> on its body, read by a velocimeter (<code>imu_velocimeter</code>) and a gyro (<code>imu_gyro</code>); and <code>policy.onnx</code> beside the model is the policy. Under a gait, and standing, the model steps as the target&#39;s other models do, has no IMU, and nothing goes beside it. A library&#39;s numbers are 0 or between 1e-100 and 1e30 in size, which keeps every number this target writes for a link finite and one MuJoCo&#39;s parser reads. Whether a robot whose quantities lie far beyond any real part&#39;s, a centre of mass 1e30 m away, steps in MuJoCo is not checked yet.</p><p><code>drive.json</code> (<code>robotdsl.mjcf_drive</code>) names, by <code>bodies</code>, the body whose frame is each component&#39;s own (<code>base_link</code> for the chassis), and says how to drive the base:</p><ul><li><p><code>&quot;kinematics&quot;: &quot;differential&quot;</code> (<code>Diff_two_caster</code>, <code>Diff_drive</code>), as <code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code>&#39;s <code>diff_drive_controller</code> drives it: each driven wheel turns at (v - w·y) / radius, its <code>y</code> half the wheel separation on the left and minus it on the right.</p></li><li><p><code>&quot;kinematics&quot;: &quot;steering&quot;</code> (<code>Ackermann</code>, <code>Tricycle</code>): the runtime&#39;s steering drive (<code>runtime/mobiledsl_runtime/steering_kinematics.py</code> in the ROS targets&#39; output), from <code>axle_x</code>, <code>min_turning_radius</code>, the <code>driven</code> wheels (<code>x</code>, <code>y</code>, <code>offset</code>, <code>radius</code>) and the <code>kingpins</code> (<code>x</code>, <code>y</code>, <code>limit</code>, <code>sign</code>): each driven wheel at the speed of its own path about the turning centre and each kingpin at its own angle, the turn held to what the kingpins can steer. That is how <code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code> drives a car. It drives a tricycle by <code>tricycle_controller</code>, which takes the front wheel on its kingpin&#39;s axis; here a tricycle is driven as a car is, a stand-in declared below.</p></li><li><p><code>&quot;kinematics&quot;: &quot;holonomic&quot;</code> (<code>Mecanum_four_wheel</code>), as <code>ros-gz-nav2</code>&#39;s <code>mecanum_drive_controller</code> drives it: each driven wheel turns at its <code>coupling</code> row times (v, v_left, w), the base&#39;s speed ahead and to the left (m/s) and its turn to the left (rad/s). The rows are the controller&#39;s, for wheels of radius r whose mounts lie a lever arm k (half the wheelbase plus half the track) from the middle along x and y: (1, -1, -k) / r front left, (1, 1, k) / r front right, (1, 1, -k) / r rear left and (1, -1, k) / r rear right.</p></li><li><p><code>&quot;kinematics&quot;: &quot;policy&quot;</code> (<code>Legged</code>): the walking controller its part states, or the walker its library trained for its body and legs. <code>policy.joints</code> are the servos in the order the policy reads and moves them: the chassis&#39;s, in the order of its links, or a walker&#39;s, in its order, each named by the program&#39;s component and link. <code>policy.file</code>, the policy beside the model, is called every <code>policy.period</code> seconds of simulated time, the model having started at the keyframe, with the terms of <code>policy.observation</code> one after another: the IMU&#39;s linear velocity in its own frame (the velocimeter <code>policy.imu.velocimeter</code>), its angular velocity (the gyro <code>policy.imu.gyro</code>), straight down in its frame (the transpose of the site <code>policy.imu.site</code>&#39;s turn applied to (0, 0, -1)); the command, ahead, to the left and turning left, within <code>policy.command</code>&#39;s ranges; each of <code>policy.joints</code>&#39; angle from its servo&#39;s <code>hold</code> and its speed, those of <code>policy.hidden</code> read as 0; the action it gave last, zeros at first; and the gait&#39;s phase (<code>policy.phase</code>), the cosine of each foot&#39;s phase and then their sines, each phase starting at its offset and turned by 2π times the frequency times the period at each call, kept between -π and π, and read as <code>still_at</code> while the command&#39;s length is under <code>still_below</code>, turning on meanwhile. Each servo&#39;s target is its <code>hold</code> plus <code>policy.action_scale</code> times the action&#39;s term for its joint, in the order of <code>policy.joints</code>. A page steps the model in two halves, <code>mj_step1</code>, the targets, <code>mj_step2</code>, as MuJoCo&#39;s control callback would.</p></li><li><p><code>&quot;kinematics&quot;: &quot;stand&quot;</code> (<code>Legged</code>): a legged base without a walking controller, its part&#39;s own or a walker its library trained for its body and legs. The model starts at the keyframe, and its servos hold their holds; nothing drives it, and <code>drive.json</code> gives no command.</p></li><li><p><code>&quot;kinematics&quot;: &quot;gait&quot;</code> (<code>Legged</code>): the gait its part states, worked out from the model itself, as the tests&#39; <code>tests/gait_walker.py</code> does. At the keyframe, after <code>mj_forward</code>, with p the chassis body&#39;s place (<code>xpos</code>), R its turn (<code>xmat</code>), g the size of gravity and h <code>gait.standing</code>, the chassis&#39;s height above the floor its feet stand on:</p><ul><li>each foot of <code>gait.feet</code> (<code>geom</code>, <code>joints</code> from its part&#39;s frame down, <code>friction</code>, <code>offset</code>) has its geom&#39;s place f; its place in the chassis frame, home = R^T (f - p); its level, l = f_z - p_z + h, its geom&#39;s height above the floor; it is on the floor, planted at (f_x, f_y, l); and its leg&#39;s angles are its hinges&#39; <code>qpos</code>. The offsets take the feet in turn about their middle as they stand, every other one half a period later;</li><li>Raibert&#39;s gain is k = sqrt(c / g), c the height of the robot&#39;s centre of mass (the chassis&#39;s <code>subtree_com</code>) above the floor, c_z - p_z + h;</li><li>the body is led from where it is: its place (p_x, p_y) and its yaw, atan2(R_10, R_00);</li><li>the gait has not started, the command it follows is zero, and the robot has settled (1).</li></ul><p>Then, with n = max(1, round(<code>gait.control</code> / timestep)), a half rounded to the even whole number as Python&#39;s <code>round</code> does, and the steps counted from 0 at the keyframe, at step 0 and at every n-th step after, between <code>mj_step1</code> and <code>mj_step2</code>, at the step&#39;s time t, the servos&#39; targets are worked out as below for the command the gait follows, (ahead, aside, turn) in the chassis frame. With c the command it is told, each part held within its range of <code>gait.command</code>, and s = (<code>gait.slew.ahead</code>, <code>gait.slew.aside</code>, <code>gait.slew.turn</code>) dt, dt as in 2, the command it follows, f, becomes f + (c - f held within -s and s), part by part, the sum worked out as written even when c is within s of f, when it is c but for its rounding. Between, the servos keep the targets they were given.</p><ol><li>The robot is settled at a working when its centre of mass moves along the floor no faster than <code>gait.settle.speed</code>, |(v_x, v_y)|, and the chassis turns no faster than <code>gait.settle.turn</code>, |w_z|, with v and w as in 2. It has settled when it is settled at this working and at the m - 1 before, m = max(1, round(<code>gait.period</code> / dt)), dt as in 2, rounded as n is; at the keyframe it counts as settled at all of them. The gait goes on while the command it follows is not zero, or while the robot has not settled. It starts at t when it goes on and has not started.</li><li>With p, R and y the chassis&#39;s place, turn and yaw now, v the velocity of the robot&#39;s centre of mass (<code>subtree_linvel</code> of the chassis, after <code>mj_subtreeVel</code>), w the chassis&#39;s turn rate (R times the free joint&#39;s last three <code>qvel</code>), v* = Rz(y) (ahead, aside, 0) and dt = n times the timestep: the body&#39;s lead place moves by v* dt and is held within <code>gait.lead.distance</code> of p, along x and along y each; its lead yaw turns by turn dt and is held within <code>gait.lead.yaw</code> of y, the difference wrapped to [-pi, pi). The body is to stand at b = (the lead place, h), turned by H = Rz(the lead yaw).</li><li>A foot&#39;s phase is 0 before the gait starts, else ((t - start) / <code>gait.period</code> + its offset) mod 1. When the gait goes on no more, has started and every phase is under <code>gait.duty</code>, it stops, and every phase is 0.</li><li>A foot is on the floor while its phase is under <code>gait.duty</code>. A foot that comes down is planted at its geom&#39;s place, at its level; a foot that comes up is lifted from its geom&#39;s place.</li><li>A foot on the floor is to be at H^T (planted - b) in the chassis frame. A foot in the air, s = (phase - duty) / (1 - duty) of its swing, lands, with a = (1 - s)(1 - duty) period + duty period / 2 and r = Rz(y) home, at L = (p_x + r_x, p_y + r_y, l) + v* a + (Rz(turn a) r - r) <ul><li>k (v - v*), the last two taken along x and y alone. It is at lifted + (L - lifted)(3 s^2 - 2 s^3) along x and y and at l + <code>gait.step_height</code> sin(pi s) along z, so at R^T (that - p) in the chassis frame.</li></ul></li><li>Each leg&#39;s angles, from its last ones, by damped least squares on the model&#39;s own kinematics (a second data: every joint as now but the free joint, whose seven <code>qpos</code> are 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, the chassis at the origin and not turned, and the leg&#39;s angles set; <code>mj_kinematics</code>, <code>mj_comPos</code>): at most 8 rounds, until the foot geom is within 2e-4 m of its place; each round adds J^T (J J^T + 1e-4 I)^-1 e, with J the geom&#39;s Jacobian in the leg&#39;s hinges (<code>mj_jacGeom</code>) and e what it misses by, and holds each angle within its hinge&#39;s range.</li><li>The balance, with m the robot&#39;s mass, f = 2 pi <code>gait.balance.frequency</code> and d = 2 <code>gait.balance.damping</code> f: the force F = m (d (v* - v)_x, d (v* - v)_y, g + f^2 (h - p_z) - d v_z); with E = H R^T, the turn&#39;s error e = (E_21 - E_12, E_02 - E_20, E_10 - E_01) / 2; and the moment M = I (f^2 e + d ((0, 0, turn) - w)), I the robot&#39;s inertia about its centre of mass c, in the world: over the chassis and every body below it, the sum of Q diag(i) Q^T + u (|o|^2 I - o o^T), with Q its <code>ximat</code>, i its <code>body_inertia</code>, u its <code>body_mass</code> and o its <code>xipos</code> less c. The feet on the floor share F and M: their forces, three each, minimise (A x - (F, M))^T W (A x - (F, M)) + 1e-3 x^T x, the rows of A summing the forces and their moments about c (the foot geom&#39;s place less c, crossed with its force), and W = diag(1, 1, 1, 10, 10, 10). Each foot&#39;s force is then held upward or zero (its z at least 0), and when its part along the floor, of length q, is more than the foot&#39;s friction mu times its z, that part is scaled by mu z / max(q, 1e-9).</li><li>Each servo of a foot&#39;s leg is told its angle, less, for a foot on the floor, (J^T x) / kp, with x the foot&#39;s force, J its geom&#39;s Jacobian in the leg&#39;s hinges on the model as it is, and kp the servo&#39;s gain, its actuator&#39;s first <code>actuator_gainprm</code>.</li></ol></li><li><p><code>&quot;kinematics&quot;: &quot;tracks&quot;</code> (<code>Tracked</code>), as <code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code>&#39;s node drives it. No joint turns: the surfaces of the belts&#39; boxes move.</p><ul><li>Once, with the model as it loads and <code>mj_kinematics</code> run on it, take every geom of each of the <code>belts</code>&#39; bodies (<code>body</code>): <code>body_geomnum</code> of them from <code>body_geomadr</code>. Keep each one&#39;s centre in the frame of the <code>root</code> body, c = R_rootᵀ (x_geom - x_root), and the rotation from that frame into its own, R_geomᵀ R_root. Tell MuJoCo that surfaces move (<code>flg_surfacevel</code>), which it skips otherwise, before stepping.</li><li>For a base motion v (m/s) and w (rad/s), take one factor s: the least <code>max_speed</code> / |v - w*c_y| over the geoms of the belts that state a <code>max_speed</code> (m/s), where that is less than 1, and otherwise 1. A null <code>max_speed</code> bounds nothing.</li><li>With v&#39; = s<em>v and w&#39; = s</em>w, each geom&#39;s <code>geom_surfacevel</code> is the linear velocity (-v&#39; + w&#39;*c_y, -w&#39;*c_x, 0) in its components 0 to 2 and the angular velocity (0, 0, -w&#39;) in 3 to 5, each rotated into the geom&#39;s frame.</li></ul><p>That is the runtime&#39;s <code>surface_motion</code> (<code>runtime/mobiledsl_runtime/track_kinematics.py</code> in the ROS targets&#39; output), as the node applies it to each command; the node also drops a command that is not finite, and stops the belts when commands stop arriving.</p></li></ul><p>Each driven wheel and kingpin names its <code>actuator</code> and <code>joint</code>; a wheel&#39;s <code>max_speed</code> is its motor&#39;s no-load speed at the wheel&#39;s joint, through whatever reduction the drive has between them, in rad/s, which bounds the joint&#39;s velocity command; null where the part states none.</p><p>A robot whose parts have links of their own names each link&#39;s body, by component and link (<code>links</code>); when some of them turn on servos, it names each servo, in the order of their actuators in the model (<code>servos</code>: its <code>joint</code>, its <code>actuator</code>, the angle it holds when nothing commands it, <code>hold</code>, and its <code>range</code>), and the keyframe that has them there (<code>keyframe</code>).</p><p>It refuses, as <code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code> does, a base on omni wheels, a moving joint on a connector a mating uses other than a wheel&#39;s own, and a robot with a part that declares neither a collision box nor a hull, other than a wheel, which collides as a sphere of its radius, and a track, which collides as its belt&#39;s box: such a part would collide through its visual mesh. Of its own it refuses a world without a floor, a mecanum wheel too light for its rollers, whose hub would be left less than 1 g, and one too wide or too narrow for them, and a legged base walking by a gait in a world whose gravity is not straight down. Its stand-ins, in <code>compile.json</code>, are that target&#39;s for the wheels, the tracks, the ground contacts and the drive, but for the odometry a tracked base&#39;s drive makes there, which this target makes none of; with these of its own:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Construct</th><th>Emitted</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A sensor</td><td>Its body, mass and collision, without its sensing</td><td>The model is the robot to drive by hand, which reads no sensor</td></tr><tr><td>The world</td><td>Its floor and its gravity, not its entities</td><td>The model is the robot to drive by hand</td></tr><tr><td>A skid-steer base</td><td>A base that turns slower than its wheels&#39; speeds command: told to turn at 0.5 rad/s while driving at 0.2 m/s, the skid-steer bases measured turned at a seventh to seven tenths of it</td><td>MuJoCo&#39;s contact friction is isotropic, so a wheel dragged across its rolling direction is resisted as much as it grips along it</td></tr><tr><td>A tricycle</td><td>Its front wheel at the speed of its own path about the turning centre on the rear axle and its kingpin at its own angle, as the steering drive drives a car&#39;s wheels</td><td><code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code> drives it by <code>tricycle_controller</code>, which takes the wheel on the kingpin&#39;s axis; the steering drive takes where the wheel and the kingpin are</td></tr><tr><td>A mecanum wheel</td><td>Its rollers round its hub, as above, said instead of a sphere contact: how many, their shape, their hand, their span, their friction and their mass</td><td>A geom&#39;s friction is the same in every direction (the model writes no contact pairs), so a wheel that grips only along its rollers rolls on rollers; the part states neither their number nor their shape</td></tr><tr><td>A part with links of its own whose links have shapes</td><td>Its links&#39; shapes with MuJoCo&#39;s default friction, 1 sliding</td><td>The part states no friction for its links&#39; shapes</td></tr><tr><td>A legged base walking by a policy</td><td>Its feet alone meeting the floor, each with its own friction, and no other shape of the robot meeting the floor or another</td><td>Its walking controller was trained on a model whose feet alone collide, and walks as it was trained</td></tr><tr><td>A legged base walking by a gait</td><td>The same</td><td>Its gait steps on its feet and holds the rest of it off the floor</td></tr><tr><td>A legged base without a walking controller</td><td>The same</td><td>It stands on its feet, as the model a walking controller is trained on, which no walking controller walks yet</td></tr><tr><td>A legged base walking by a policy, carrying other parts</td><td>Its walking controller carrying them</td><td>The controller was trained on the chassis alone, so their mass is one it was not trained with</td></tr><tr><td>A link lighter than 1 g, or with a principal moment the decomposition finds less than 1e-6 kg m²</td><td>The link as heavy as 1 g, and each such principal moment 1e-6 kg m², as the model writes them</td><td>MuJoCo&#39;s compiler raises a body to the least mass and moment the model allows, the bounds the <code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code> target sets</td></tr></tbody></table>`,59)])])}const u=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{p as __pageData,u as default};
